DEBUG: say what was actually opened when a file is not found.

    tcc: error: file 'crt1.o' not found

That names the file and not one of the places it was sought. From the outside
"the search path is wrong" and "the file really is absent" look identical, and
they need opposite fixes. Several rounds of this spike went into guessing
which -- reproducing the failure, reasoning about multiarch triplets, changing
configure flags, re-reading the same unchanged log line each time. s->crt_paths
held the answer throughout and nothing ever printed it.

With it, one run settled what four rounds of argument had not:

    tcc: error: file 'crt1.o' not found
            tried: /usr/lib/arm64-linux-gnu/crt1.o

-- a directory Debian does not have, because -D TCC_TARGET_ARM64 on the
command line skipped the config.h block that defines CONFIG_TRIPLET.

It prints the full CANDIDATE NAME rather than the directory, because a
directory list still leaves two situations looking alike: the file is absent,
or it is present and the open failed. "library 'c' not found" over a directory
that does contain libc.so cannot be explained from the log without it.

An EMPTY path list is called out separately. Zero paths and a wrong path are
indistinguishable in the original message and mean different things: zero means
the CONFIG_* macros expanded to nothing.

A tcc-debug patch: the micro-c copy only, never the control, so the comparison
stays honest.

diff --git a/libtcc.c b/libtcc.c
index 4d1d829..8fb2e54 100644
--- a/libtcc.c
+++ b/libtcc.c
@@ -1295,9 +1295,39 @@ static int tcc_add_library_internal(TCCState *s1, const char *fmt,
         if (ret != FILE_NOT_FOUND)
             return ret;
     }
-    if (flags & AFF_PRINT_ERROR)
+    if (flags & AFF_PRINT_ERROR) {
         tcc_error_noabort("%s '%s' not found",
             flags & AFF_TYPE_LIB ? "library" : "file", filename);
+        /* SAY WHAT WAS ACTUALLY OPENED.
+         *
+         * "file 'crt1.o' not found" names the file and not one of the places
+         * it was sought, so the log cannot tell "the search path is wrong"
+         * from "the file really is missing" -- and those need opposite fixes.
+         * Several rounds of this spike went into guessing which: reproducing
+         * the failure, reasoning about multiarch triplets, changing configure
+         * flags, and re-reading the same unchanged line each time. s->crt_paths
+         * held the answer throughout and nothing ever printed it.
+         *
+         * The full candidate name is printed, not the directory, because a
+         * directory list still leaves two different situations looking alike:
+         * absent, or present and the open failed. "library 'c' not found" over
+         * a directory that does contain libc.so is unexplainable without it.
+         *
+         * An EMPTY list is called out separately: zero paths and a wrong path
+         * are indistinguishable in the old message and mean different things.
+         * Zero means the CONFIG_* macros expanded to nothing -- which is
+         * exactly what -D TCC_TARGET_ARM64 on the command line caused, by
+         * skipping the config.h block that defines CONFIG_TRIPLET. */
+        if (nb_paths == 0) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "        (no search paths at all -- the path"
+                            " macros expanded to nothing)\n");
+        } else {
+            for (i = 0; i < nb_paths; i++) {
+                snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), fmt, paths[i], filename);
+                fprintf(stderr, "        tried: %s\n", buf);
+            }
+        }
+    }
     return FILE_NOT_FOUND;
 }
 
